# Project artifacts

Create this tree in the work repository, not in the user's profile. Do not create an empty file without useful content. For a trivial change, do not create the tree.

```
docs/
├── product/PRD.md
├── architecture/ARCHITECTURE.md
├── architecture/ADR/
├── specs/
├── tests/
├── tasks/
├── changes/
├── reviews/
├── security/
└── progress/
    ├── CURRENT.md
    └── ROADMAP.md
```

- `docs/product/PRD.md` — problem, outcome, requirements, out of scope
- `docs/architecture/ARCHITECTURE.md` — current view of the system
- `docs/architecture/ADR/NNNN-title.md` — one decision; a new ADR supersedes the old one
- `docs/specs/` — what the system must do, linked to requirements
- `docs/tests/` — verification strategy; executable tests live in the project's suite
- `docs/tasks/` — verifiable steps, with explicit dependencies
- `docs/changes/` — change requests during implementation
- `docs/reviews/<slug>.md` — review with status and FIND-NNN findings, read by the gate
- `docs/security/<slug>.md` or `security_assessment.md` — security report with SEC-NNN findings
- Proofs (`check_proofs.py`) live in `.cursor/agent-system/proofs/`, not in `docs/`
- Same feature, same file name (slug) in specs, tasks, tests, reviews, and security. `layout.json` (`overrides`) changes the folders.
- `docs/progress/CURRENT.md` — current checkpoint
- `docs/progress/ROADMAP.md` — phases and tasks with status; only in an initialized project with M or larger work (`roadmap` skill)

If `.cursor/agent-system/layout.json` exists, it is the map of what the project already uses. Write new ADRs, PRDs, and specs to those paths. Do not move or rewrite the files listed there.

If the repository already has another canonical place for specs or ADRs, use the existing one and do not duplicate the tree.

## Repository outside the standard

Do not rebuild the PRD, architecture, or tasks for what is already done. Do not create an empty file to "complete" the tree.

On the next non-trivial work:

- Write the spec and acceptance criteria only for what will change.
- Write the verification strategy for that change.
- Write `docs/progress/CURRENT.md` (or the equivalent already used in the repo) with the state of this front.
- Open an ADR only if this change decides architecture.
- Open a PRD or `ARCHITECTURE.md` only if the change is large enough to need them, or if the user asks to adopt the standard.

The tree grows with new work. The old code remains valid as it is until someone changes that behavior.
